  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I typically spend most of my time in vrchat with a user who has some physical disabilities. They are on Quest. Here's a few of the things they struggle with after several weeks of gathering data:\n\nKeybinds both allowing combinations and remaps would be very welcome in quest. They cannot press both triggers at the same time or use a grip and trigger in combination, or hold the grip for an extended period of time. \nremapping trigger to a thumb button, for example, would be AMAZING. \n\nfor context: Button holding (for some buttons) is okay if it's a single button, but holding one and pressing another is hard. She has dexterous use of about 2 fingers on each hand (primarily thumb and index) and cannot fully hold and use a controller with her left, it requires two hands or to be placed on her lap. \n\nDue to the way she is seated, their hands are always in front of their face in standing and seated mode. It would be nice to be able to set a button or option to \"rest position\" the hands to the sides just for visual appeal.\n\nThey have no way to crouch reliably IRL so they cannot reach things on the floor or even half height if the collision is small (beer pong for example). A physical button, which cycles through crouch and prone would be great, like on desktop. \n\nThe raycast for selecting objects is very precise and can be very problematic on small interaction meshes. \n\nCyan's climbing prefab is great, but she cannot hold the grips for a long time, and cannot hold grip and trigger except on one hand. If it's possible to set up a \"toggle to hold\" as a game-wide setting, it would help a ton in cases like this.\n\nThe ability to use the in game menu as a floating menu but interact with it on the same hand rather than double handed, since using both hands to select things is tougher for her. One handed users would find it even harder.\n\nFinally, the ability to set height to something very small might make sense since from a chair or bed your \"height\" can be quite low.",
